        "1.2 The point at which thinking stops\nA common pattern is:\n1. You encounter a problem.\n2. You think about it using what you already know.\n3. You reach the edge of your current understanding.\n4. Instead of exploring beyond that edge, you retreat to the explanation you already trust.\n5. You defend that explanation as if it were the only possible answer.\nAt this point, thought becomes repetition. You may be repeating what your family taught you, what your professional group believes, what an authority said, or what you have seen repeatedly online.",
        "The limitation is not that you have a perspective. Everyone does. The limitation is forgetting that your perspective is partial.",
        "1.3 A working definition of strategic or “genius” thinking\nIn this framework, strong thinking means being able to:\n- Keep unfamiliar or challenging ideas within the realm of possibility long enough to examine them.\n- Prefer understanding over simply collecting facts.\n- Move between perspectives without becoming trapped in one.\n- Connect ideas from different domains.\n- Produce a coherent explanation, useful decision, or original insight from those connections.\nThe width, depth, and height of your thinking describe how far you can explore without prematurely closing the inquiry.",
        "This does not mean every idea deserves equal belief. Openness is not credulity. It means examining an idea before rejecting it, then evaluating it according to evidence, logic, context, and consequences.",
        "1.4 The “smart but stuck” problem\nDomain expertise is valuable, but it can create blind spots when it becomes the only lens available.\n| Person | Strong domain | Possible blind spot |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Successful business owner | Strategy, sales, operations | Meaning, relationships, identity after business |\n| Skilled creative | Craft and originality | Customer demand, pricing, distribution, business models |\n| Physically disciplined person | Training and nutrition | Emotional intelligence, communication, relationships |\n| Technical specialist | Engineering and systems | Human behaviour, incentives, culture, adoption |",
