        "Part II — The First Three Dimensions",
        "3.1 What a line represents\nA line of thinking is your development within one specific domain.",
        "It could be marketing, engineering, finance, nutrition, politics, history, psychology, relationships, or any other field. As you gain knowledge and experience in that domain, your line becomes wider.",
        "Think of it as progression in a skill tree or a video game:\n- At the beginning, you are unfamiliar with the subject.\n- You learn its basic concepts and vocabulary.\n- You practise common methods.\n- You gain experience and recognise patterns.\n- You become capable of handling more complex situations.\nThe line describes domain-specific development. It does not, by itself, tell you how sophisticated your overall thinking is.",
        "3.2 Build depth without becoming trapped\nSpecialisation is not the enemy. Deep expertise can be essential. The problem begins when expertise turns into the assumption that one domain explains everything.",
        "A specialist should be able to say:\n- “This is what my field explains well.”\n- “This is where my field has limits.”\n- “This part of the problem may require another perspective.”\n- “I need more evidence before applying this model.”\nThat is how expertise remains useful without becoming dogma.",
